.\" Manual page for gputop.
.\"
.\" Every option documented here is registered by build_parser() in src/gputop/cli.py and
.\" every default quoted here is the dataclass default in src/gputop/config.py.  The
.\" interactive keys are the BINDINGS list in src/gputop/ui/app.py.
.TH gputop 1 "2026-09-30" "gputop 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gputop \- a terminal monitor for AMD Radeon GPUs driven by the amdgpu kernel driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gputop
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B gputop
.B \-\-dump
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B gputop
.B \-\-devices
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B gputop
.B \-\-check
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B gputop \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gputop
shows what the Radeon cards in this machine are doing: utilisation, clocks, temperatures,
power draw against the enforced cap, throttle reasons, and the DRM clients holding a
descriptor, with the GPU time and memory attributed to each of them. With no arguments it
starts a Textual interface that updates in place. The panels are laid out to survive a
narrow or a short terminal, and the last two of them are conditional, because a panel
that is merely turned off should say nothing at all.
.PP
Everything is read from
.IR /sys/class/drm/card*/device/
and from
.IR /proc/<pid>/fdinfo/ ,
and nothing is ever written to either. A monitor that quietly reprograms a GPU is not a
monitor, so there is no write path in this program: the power-profile and overdrive tables
are read because they are interesting, not because they can be set.
.PP
The interface itself is unprivileged. The one exception is the optional per-block panel:
the amdgpu driver exposes its per-block busy counters only through a privileged radeon
ioctl, so when it is enabled
.B gputop
runs
.B radeontop \-d \-
as a child process and parses its dump output. That child inherits whatever privilege
.B radeontop
needs, and
.B gputop
itself never does. The panel is off by default, because a default-on panel would put a
permanent complaint in the status line of every user whose
.B radeontop
answers "are you root?", and because opting in stops
.B gputop
from launching a process that reaches for the GPU the moment it starts.
.PP
Three modes exist for scripts and bug reports rather than for watching:
.B \-\-devices
lists the cards,
.B \-\-dump
writes one JSON snapshot of the live machine,
and
.B \-\-check
reports which metrics this machine can provide, and why any of them are missing. Each of
the three prints to stdout and exits.
.PP
Requirements: Linux with
.BR CONFIG_DRM_AMDGPU ,
kernel 5.19 or newer, and Python 3.14 (standard or free-threaded). Kernel 5.19 is the
floor because that is the release which gave
.I /proc/<pid>/fdinfo
its DRM accounting keys
.RB ( drm-client-id ,
.BR drm-engine-* ,
.BR drm-memory-* ),
which is the whole basis of the per-process table. The binary
.I gpu_metrics
table arrived later, in 6.10; without it the interface still works, one sysfs attribute at
a time, and
.B gputop \-\-check
says so rather than leaving you to guess why one number is missing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-c \fIPATH\fR, \-\-config \fIPATH\fR
Read the configuration from
.I PATH
instead of searching the default locations described under CONFIGURATION. Naming a file
also means naming the whole configuration: the saved
.B [state]
section is not layered on top of it, so a scripted run cannot inherit whatever the last
interactive session happened to leave behind.
.TP
.B \-i \fISECONDS\fR, \-\-interval \fISECONDS\fR
Sample every
.I SECONDS
instead of every
.BR general.interval_ms .
An interval below 0.05 s is raised to it: a faster tick would spend more time in procfs
than collecting data, and the procfs scan is the expensive part of a sample.
.TP
.B \-\-drm\-root \fIPATH\fR
Take the sysfs DRM class directory from
.I PATH
rather than
.BR /sys/class/drm .
Useful for testing against a captured tree.
.TP
.B \-\-proc\-root \fIPATH\fR
Take the procfs mount point from
.I PATH
rather than
.BR /proc .
Useful for the same reason.
.TP
.B \-\-dump
Write one JSON snapshot of the live machine to stdout and exit. Engine utilisation is a
difference between two samples, so this takes two samples with one interval between them;
without the wait every engine would read 0%. Optional readings are emitted as
.B null
rather than omitted, so a consumer can tell "not available on this hardware" apart from
"not collected".
.TP
.B \-\-devices
Write a JSON list of the discovered GPUs to stdout and exit, with the PCI address of each
card. That address is the key
.B [gpu.names]
uses to rename a card, and the card number here is the one the interface shows.
.TP
.BI \-\-kind " {auto,igpu,dgpu}"
Classify every device as integrated or discrete regardless of what the heuristics decide.
.I auto
leaves them to the heuristics;
.I igpu
and
.I dgpu
force the answer, which is what an APU the probes misjudge needs.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " NAME"
Use the colour theme
.IR NAME :
.BR default ,
.B dracula
or
.B gruvbox
(the default is
.BR default ).
The same key,
.BR m ,
cycles the theme at run time, and the choice is remembered in the
.B [state]
section.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable 24-bit colour and fall back to the terminal's own ANSI palette. The same setting
is
.BR ui.no_color .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-processes
Skip the
.B /proc
scan, which is the most expensive part of a sample. The process table is then empty and
every other panel still works, which is the point when the sampler is running on a timer
or a machine with many processes.
.TP
.B \-\-pretty
Indent
.B \-\-dump
and
.B \-\-devices
output for human reading. Not for parsing.
.TP
.B \-\-blocks
Force the per-block panel on for this run, overriding
.BR blocks.enabled ,
and start
.B radeontop
if it is installed. When it cannot run, the panel is hidden rather than drawn empty and the
status line names which of the four reasons applies: not installed, not permitted,
unsupported card, or failed.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-blocks
Never start
.BR radeontop ,
even when it is installed, whatever
.B blocks.enabled
says.
.B \-\-blocks
and
.B \-\-no\-blocks
are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.B \-\-log \fIPATH\fR
Record every sample to
.IR PATH ,
appending and flushing per record so an interrupted session still leaves a usable file.
The suffix before any compression suffix picks the format and the trailing suffix picks
the transport, so the two are independent:
.B .csv
is CSV,
.B .json
is JSON Lines (one object per line, one per GPU),
.B .csv.zst
is the same CSV compressed. A target that cannot be opened is reported as a warning and
recording is skipped: recording a session is not a reason to refuse to watch one.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Report which metrics this machine can provide and why any are missing, then exit. The
report is written for a bug report: a card that exposes no junction sensor, or a kernel
without the binary metrics table, produces a permanent
.B N/A
on screen, and this is how you find out which file is the reason before asking.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit the
.B \-\-check
report as JSON instead of text. It has no effect without
.BR \-\-check ,
which is the only thing that produces a report.
.SH KEYS
These apply to the interface, which is what
.B gputop
starts with no arguments. A program that takes keys has no room for a key reference
beside it.
.TP
.B q
Quit. The
.B [state]
section is flushed on the way out, and periodically while you watch, so the next start
looks the way this one was left.
.TP
.B tab
Next GPU. Binds ahead of the interface's own focus traversal, which would otherwise make
the most-used multi-GPU key move the cursor.
.TP
.B shift+tab
Previous GPU.
.TP
.B o
Overview: every GPU at once instead of one at a time.
.TP
.B 1
to
.BR 9 ,
Jump to GPU
.IR n .
.TP
.B /
Filter the process table by name, command, user or pid.
.TP
.B h ", " \&?
Help. Also repeats the reason when an optional panel is not on screen.
.TP
.B R
Resample now, instead of waiting for the next interval.
.TP
.B +
Sample more often, in steps of 100 ms, down to 100 ms.
.TP
.B \-
Sample less often, in the same steps, up to 10 s.
.TP
.B m
Cycle the colour theme. The choice is saved.
.TP
.B p
Show or hide the process table.
.TP
.B s
Sort by the next column.
.TP
.B S
Sort by the previous column. The selection follows the process rather than the row, so it
stays on the same client as the table reorders.
.TP
.B r
Reverse the sort.
.TP
.B escape
Clear the filter.
.TP
.B a
Show or hide idle clients: processes holding a DRM descriptor but using nothing measurable.
Off by default, because such a row answers none of the questions this table exists for.
.TP
.B t
Show the process table as a parent/child tree.
.TP
.B c
Show the full
.B /proc/<pid>/cmdline
instead of the 15-character
.B comm
name.
.TP
.B up ", " \&down
Move the selection.
.TP
.B k
Send a signal to the selected process, chosen from a confirmation dialog that names the
target. This key does nothing unless
.B process.allow_kill
is set, because terminating another process from a monitor is one keystroke away from
terminating the wrong one. A kernel thread, PID 1 and
.B gputop
itself are refused even then.
.SH FILES
All of these are read-only, and none of them requires privilege.
.TP
.B /sys/class/drm/card*/device/
Per-card attributes. The directory is matched by the name
.B cardN
alone, so connector nodes such as
.B card1-DP-1
are not mistaken for cards. A card is accepted only when its
.B driver
symlink points at
.BR amdgpu ;
the older
.B radeon
driver and every other vendor are skipped silently.
.TP
.B gpu_metrics
The versioned binary metric table (v1.x and v2.x), and the source of busy percentage,
clocks, power and temperatures when the kernel provides it.
.TP
.B mem_info_vis_vram_used ", " mem_info_vis_vram_total ", " mem_info_vram_total ", " mem_info_gtt_used ", " mem_info_gtt_total
Memory pools. An integrated GPU is judged by its GTT pool, so the memory panel leads with
GTT and de-emphasises VRAM when the card is an APU.
.TP
.B pp_dpm_sclk ", " pp_dpm_mclk ", " pp_dpm_socclk ", " pp_dpm_performance_level
DPM clock tables and the current performance level. The direct
.B current_sclk
and
.B current_mclk
attributes are widely documented and absent on amdgpu, so these tables are normally the
source, and the row marked for the active state is what is shown;
.B gpu_metrics
is preferred over both when the kernel exposes it.
.TP
.B current_* ", " pp_dpm_force_performance_level ", " current_link_width ", " current_link_speed
Live clock, forced performance level, link width and link speed.
.TP
.B pp_power_profile_mode
The SCPP profile table: the active profile, its index, and the alternatives the driver
offers. Read, never written.
.TP
.B pp_od_clk_voltage
Each clock domain's overdrive ceiling and the vddgfx offset. Also read, never written.
.TP
.B hwmon/hwmon*/
Temperatures, power draw, power cap and fan. Resolved through the card's own
.B hwmon
symlink rather than by scanning
.BR /sys/class/hwmon/ ,
because only
.B hwmon/name
identifies the class and not the owning device.
.TP
.B /proc/<pid>/fdinfo/
Per-process DRM client accounting: which card, which client id, how much VRAM and GTT it
holds, and the per-engine busy nanoseconds the driver accumulates. Kernel threads are
hidden because they have no argument vector, cannot be signalled, and are rarely what
anyone is looking for.
.TP
.B /dev/dri
Only consulted when the blocks panel is on, so that
.B radeontop \-p
can be given a DRM node. A node is preferred over a PCI bus number because the bus alone
is ambiguous when two cards sit behind the same bridge.
.SH CONFIGURATION
TOML, read with the standard library's
.IR tomllib ,
so no parser is installed to read it. The first of these that exists is used:
.IP \(bu 2
.BR $GPUTOP_CONFIG
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ~/.config/gputop/gputop.toml
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ~/.gputoprc
.PP
A file given with
.B \-c
replaces the search and is read on its own. Unknown keys and out-of-range values are
collected as warnings and ignored rather than raised, so a file written for a newer release
still starts today. Values are clamped, not rejected, because a working configuration with
a complaint in the status line is more useful than a refusal.
.PP
Each key below is optional and shown with its default.
.TP
.B [general]
\fIinterval_ms\fR = 1000, \fIhistory_points\fR = 300. The sampling interval in
milliseconds and the number of samples retained for the graphs; at one second the default
retention is five minutes of history.
.TP
.B [gpu]
\fIdevices\fR = (), \fIkind\fR = "auto", \fInames\fR = {}. Which
cards to show, how to classify them, and a table of
marketing names for cards the built-in table does not know, keyed by the PCI address that
.B \-\-devices
prints. A card is named by its full PCI address, the same address without the domain, or
its
.B cardN
sysfs node; an entry matching no card is reported and ignored, and a filter matching none
at all watches every card rather than none.
.TP
.B [process]
\fIshow\fR = true, \fImax_rows\fR = 20, \fIsort\fR = "total",
\fImin_usage_percent\fR = 0.0, \fIhide_kernel_threads\fR = true,
\fIprocess_tree\fR = false,
\fIfull_command\fR = false, \fIshow_idle\fR = false, \fIallow_kill\fR = false. Which
column to sort by, how many rows to keep, and whether the table may signal a process. There
is no flag for this section; it is a choice made once rather than per session.
.TP
.B [ui]
\fItheme\fR = "default", \fIno_color\fR = false, \fIgraph_height\fR = 0. Appearance and
graph sizing.
.I graph_height
is rows in the utilisation graph, where 0 -- the default -- lets it fill whatever the layout
can spare. A positive value pins it to that many rows, clamped down rather than allowed to
overflow the terminal. How many points each graph holds is
.IR general.history_points ,
and there is deliberately no second key for it: a graph cannot hold more samples than the
sampler retains for it.
.TP
.B [blocks]
\fIenabled\fR = false, \fIbinary\fR = "radeontop", \fIticks\fR = 120,
\fIinterval_s\fR = 1, \fIdrm_root\fR = "/dev/dri", \fIrestart\fR = true. The optional
per-block panel.
.I interval_s
is whole seconds only, because
.B radeontop
parses
.B \-i
with
.IR atoi (3)
and silently floors it at one; clamping here rather than honouring the request keeps the
panel from updating four times less often than the file asked for with nothing saying so.
.I restart
retries a child that dies mid-session, and does not retry a missing binary or a refused
ioctl, because it will refuse again just as fast.
.TP
.B [alerts]
\fIenabled\fR = true, \fItemp_c\fR = 90.0, \fIpower_percent\fR = 95.0,
\fIvram_percent\fR = 92.0, \fIflash_hz\fR = 1.0. Thresholds for temperature, power
against the cap, and VRAM in use. The thresholds are absolute rather than derived because
there is no honest way to derive "too hot" from what the card reports: the junction limit
is a property of the silicon, not something sysfs states. A threshold of 0 disables that
one check; a missing reading never alerts; every GPU is judged, not only the focused one.
\fIflash_hz\fR = 0 holds a steady border instead of flashing, which is not the same as
switching alerting off.
.TP
.B [log]
\fIzstd_level\fR = 3, \fIinterval_s\fR = 0.0. How a
.B \-\-log
recording is written. Nothing here turns recording on; only
.B \-\-log
does. \fIinterval_s\fR = 0 records every sample, because a recording with gaps in it is a
recording that lost samples, and choosing to lose them is yours to make.
.TP
.B [state]
Machine-written. It records the choices made while watching rather than the settings
decided in advance: \fItheme\fR, \fIinterval_ms\fR, \fIprocess_sort\fR,
\fIprocess_sort_reverse\fR, \fIprocess_filter\fR, \fIshow_idle_processes\fR,
\fIshow_process_tree\fR, \fIfull_command\fR, \fIoverview\fR, \fIgpu_index\fR. It lives in
the same file as the configuration, so the next start looks the way the last one was left.
Editing it by hand works, but deleting the section is the supported way back to the values
written above: nothing else in the file is ever touched, and the write is atomic, so a
crash halfway through cannot truncate the configuration.
.PP
Precedence, lowest to highest: built-in defaults, the config file, the saved
.B [state]
section, the command line. The command line comes last, which is why a one-off
.B gputop \-i 200
still wins over what the last session settled on.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GPUTOP_CONFIG
Path of the configuration file to use. It is searched first, so it overrides both files in
the home directory; naming a nonexistent path is a warning, not an error.
.TP
.B GPUTOP_STATE
Path of the file the
.B [state]
section is read from and written to. It exists so that a test, a container or a second
profile can keep its state separate instead of quietly inheriting whatever the last session
chose.
.PP
These two are the whole list. Colour is not taken from
.BR $TERM ,
.BR $COLORTERM
or
.BR $NO_COLOR :
.B \-\-no\-color
and
.B ui.no_color
decide that, so a redirect produces the same output regardless of the environment.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested work finished. On
.BR \-\-dump ,
.B \-\-devices
and
.BR \-\-check ,
this also means at least one
.B amdgpu
card was found.
.TP
.B 3
No AMD GPU was found
.RB ( EXIT_NO_DEVICE ).
Only the three one-shot modes distinguish this from success, so a script can tell "no
hardware" from "gputop is broken". The interface exits 0 in this case as well: it can still
show a card that another program is holding open, and a window with an error on it is more
useful than a terminal that vanished.
.TP
.B 2
The command line could not be parsed, by the argument parser's own convention.
.SH EXAMPLES
Watch the machine, with the interface deciding where its colours go:
.EX
gputop
.EE
Watch at half the default rate, one GPU at a time, no idle clients:
.EX
gputop \-i 0.5 \-\-no\-blocks
.EE
List the cards and print the PCI address of each, to paste into
.B [gpu.names]:
.EX
gputop \-\-devices \-\-pretty
.EE
Pipe one sample into
.BR jq (1):
.EX
gputop \-\-dump | jq '.devices[].temperatures'
.EE
Record a session as compressed CSV, then watch:
.EX
gputop \-\-log ~/session.csv.zst
.EE
Log one snapshot a minute from cron, with the process scan left out:
.EX
gputop \-\-dump \-\-no\-processes \-\-pretty > /var/tmp/gpu-$(date +%s).json
.EE
Find out which sensor or counter a panel's
.B N/A
is waiting on, as text or as JSON to keep with the bug report:
.EX
gputop \-\-check
gputop \-\-check \-\-json > gpu-support.json
.EE
Install the command:
.EX
pipx install gputop
uv tool install gputop
.EE
On Arch, from the PKGBUILD that ships in the source tree under
.IR packaging/arch :
.EX
cd packaging/arch && makepkg \-si
.EE
